                "event": "Flash Flood Warning",
                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Sioux Falls SD",
                "headline": "Flash Flood Warning issued September 6 at 1:37PM CDT until September 6 at 4:00PM CDT by NWS Sioux Falls SD",
                "description": "At 137 PM CDT, Doppler radar and automated rain gauges indicated\nthunderstorms producing heavy rain moving back into the area from the\nwest, especially near the Minnesota-Iowa state line from Ellsworth to\nBigelow. Between 2 and 3.5 inches of rain have fallen across the\nwarned area. The expected rainfall rate is 1 to 3 inches in 1 hour.\nAdditional rainfall amounts of 1.5 to 3 inches are possible in the\nwarned area. Flash flooding is ongoing or expected to begin shortly.\n\nHAZARD...Flash flooding caused by thunderstorms.\n\nSOURCE...Radar and automated gauges.\n\nIMPACT...Flash flooding of small creeks and streams, urban areas,\nhighways, streets and underpasses as well as other poor\ndrainage and low-lying areas.\n\nSome locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nWorthington, Ellsworth, Little Rock, Rushmore and Bigelow.",
                "instruction": "Turn around, don't drown when encountering flooded roads. Most flood\ndeaths occur in vehicles.\n\nBe aware of your surroundings and do not drive on flooded roads.\n\nFlooding is occurring or is imminent. It is important to know where\nyou are relative to streams, rivers, or creeks which can become\nkillers in heavy rains. Campers and hikers should avoid streams or\ncreeks.\n\nPlease report observed flooding to local emergency services or law\nenforcement and request they pass this information to the National\nWeather Service when you can do so safely.",
                "response": "Avoid",
                "note": null,
                "parameters": {
                    "AWIPSidentifier": [
                        "FFSFSD"
                    ],
                    "WMOidentifier": [
                        "WGUS73 KFSD 061837"
                    ],
                    "NWSheadline": [
